  "query_preview": "..Southwesterly flow will continue to strengthen\nacross the region ahead of a trough currently pushing into the\nPacNW. Gusts this afternoon are expected to reach up to 35 mph\nacross typically prone areas such as western valleys south of I-80\nand across Uinta Co., WY. Tomorrow, as this trough approaches,\nsouthwest flow will further increase though still remain under\nadvisory criteria around 40 mph in these same areas. High-res\nguidance suggests an initial boundary will develop across northern\nUtah as a distant shortwave trough rotates through the mid-level\nlow, which will result in somewhat lighter, westerly flow around\nthe Salt Lake Valley northward. As a stronger shortwave passes\nthrough the main low Sunday evening and overnight, this will bring\nthe main cold front through northern and central Utah, resulting\nin colder temperatures and a switch to gusty northwesterly winds. \nMonday highs will reach 5-10 degrees below normal across northern\nareas (near normal across central/southern Utah).\n\nLingering moisture across southeastern Utah will result in\nisolated thunderstorms developing this afternoon between mainly\nLake Powell up into the Uinta Basin. Given that this moisture is\nrelatively shallow in the mid-levels, the main threat will be\ngusty outflow winds, with just an isolated flash flood threat\ngiven any training storms over a sensitive area. Southwest flow \nwill potentially draw a bit more moisture over the state on \nSunday, however model guidance shows higher uncertainty in the \nmagnitude of this moisture. Similar to today, the flash flood \nthreat will still remain fairly isolated despite slightly higher\nspatial coverage of storms, particularly considering quicker \nstorm motions.\n\nThe cold front will struggle to scour the moisture out of\nsoutheastern Utah as it dissipates partway through the state, thus\nexpect isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms to develop\neach afternoon this week. Across the rest of the area, expect dry\nconditions and gradually warming temperatures.\n\nUncertainty increases greatly by mid-week, with potential for \nanother shortwave trough drawing moisture northward across Utah \nlate Wednesday into Thursday. Around 30% of ensemble members \nsuggest this shortwave does not develop, which would likely yield \nquieter conditions.",
